+# 11. Government-Project Readiness and Compliance Evidence
+
+These are engineering controls for a public-sector system. They do not certify legal compliance, an information-system security level, accessibility conformance, or production acceptance.
+
+## 11.1. Authority and evidence
+
+- Verify every legal citation against the official source and record its identifier, effective version/date, applicable scope, and requirement mapping.
+- Use the approved security-level dossier, data/account/retention policies, and acceptance criteria when supplied; do not infer their contents.
+- Track each control as `Not assessed`, `Gap`, `Implemented`, `Verified`, or `Accepted exception`. Only an authorized reviewer may set `Verified` or accept a time-bound exception.
+- Evidence must identify the implementation, test/configuration/operational artifact, owner, reviewer, date, and remaining limitation.
+
+## 11.2. Data governance and accountability
+
+- Classify public, internal, personal, sensitive, confidential, and state-secret data under the approved scheme.
+- Record purpose, owner, access/organization scope, storage, sharing destination, retention, disposal, and legal hold.
+- Minimize and mask data in APIs, UI, exports, reports, logs, audit records, and non-production environments.
+- Enforce separation of duties for administration, approval, publication, signing, audit review, and exception approval where applicable.
+
+## 11.3. Public service and interoperability
+
+- Public UI must support semantic HTML, keyboard operation, visible focus, labels, contrast, screen readers, reduced motion, responsive layouts, Vietnamese-first content, and approved localization.
+- Do not claim a named accessibility level until it is tested against the approved baseline.
+- Use versioned contracts, stable identifiers, idempotency, correlation IDs, documented error semantics, and server-controlled integration destinations.
+
+## 11.4. Operational acceptance
+
+Before production acceptance, collect environment evidence for threat modelling, dependency/vulnerability review, TLS and secret/key rotation, least-privilege deployment, monitoring, incident response, backup/restore, approved RPO/RTO, rollback, and disaster-recovery exercises.
+
+Open critical risks require remediation or explicit, accountable, time-bound acceptance. Local code tests never prove production compliance.
